INT. HAND CAR WASH - BAY 2 - NIGHT

A concrete tunnel hosed down for the night. Squeegees on hooks, foam drying on the walls, a radio playing low cumbia. A black sedan is parked in the middle, suds still sliding off its hood.

VICTOR "VIC" ALMEIDA (40s, rubber apron, tattoo of a swallow on his neck) wrings out a chamois. LORETTA SPENCE (50s, owner, reading glasses, bank bag on her hip) counts bills on an upturned bucket. PAULIE OKAFOR-REYES (20s, new kid, headphones around his neck) dries the rims one at a time.

VIC
Loretta, you want me to lock the front?

LORETTA
In a minute.

PAULIE
Boss, is this guy coming back for his car?

VIC
Mind your rims.

PAULIE
He left it two hours ago. He said ten minutes.

LORETTA
Paulie, count the tip jar for me.

PAULIE
There's only eleven bucks.

VIC
That's eleven more than the last time.

Loretta looks up, smiling, folds the bills into the bag, snaps it shut. Paulie opens the trunk to wipe the seals. Vic hangs a squeegee on its hook and crosses to the door and drops the latch; Loretta watches him do it and laughs lightly at a joke only she remembers; Paulie freezes with a rag in one hand and stares into the trunk.

PAULIE
Vic.

VIC
What?

PAULIE
Vic, there's a guy in here.

Loretta stops laughing. Vic walks back, slow. The three of them look down into the dark trunk. Inside, a MAN in a suit lies on his side, eyes open, a neat hole in his collar.

LORETTA
Close it.

PAULIE
He's dead. Boss, he's dead.

VIC
Paulie. Close it.

LORETTA
Vic, you said Dominguez would pick it up in the morning.

VIC
That's what he told me.

LORETTA
Then why is it in my bay?

PAULIE
Whose guy is that?

LORETTA
Nobody's.

Headlights sweep the front window. A car idles outside. The radio plays on. Loretta lays the bank bag on the bucket, wipes her hands on her slacks, and turns to face the door.
